<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="dishwasher-repair/samsung-not-draining"
  eyebrow="Samsung Dishwasher Not Draining · 5C / 5E / SC / OC · Same Day Southern California"
  title="Samsung Dishwasher Not Draining: 5C, 5E, SC"
  subtitle="5C, 5E, SC, and SE all mean 'can't drain.' Usually the filter, a pinched hose, the disposal, or air gap. Here's the order to check and when to call."
  heroImageTitle="Samsung Dishwasher Not Draining"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Samsung won't drain? Filter, then the hose it's sitting on.</h2>

    <p><strong>Standing water in a Samsung dishwasher shows as 5C, 5E, SC, or SE — all the same "can't drain" code — and almost always traces to a clogged filter and sump, a kinked or pinched drain hose (very often the dishwasher is literally sitting on its own hose), a disposal knockout plug after a new disposal, a blocked sink air gap, or a failed drain pump. The first four are safe to check yourself; the pump is where our techs take over. Start by cleaning the filter and checking that the hose isn't crushed behind the unit. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>Samsung dishwashers drain through the kitchen sink and, in most homes, the garbage disposal, so many "Samsung won't drain" calls our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair take turn out to be a packed filter or a pinched hose rather than a broken machine. Right order first.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 CODES -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · The codes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">5C, 5E, SC, SE — and how OC is different.</h2>

    <p><strong>5C, 5E, SC, and SE are one code: "the dishwasher can't drain."</strong> The characters differ only by display font — 5 and S look alike, and so do C and E — so don't read them as four separate faults. The fix is the drain path: clean the filter and sump, check the drain hose for a kink or pinch, clear the disposal and air gap, and pull the disposal knockout plug on a new install.</p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>OC (or oE / 0C) is a different code.</strong> It's an over-level water fault — the unit senses more water than there should be, which a slow or partly blocked drain can cause. The checks overlap with the drain list, but OC is a water-level warning, not the "can't drain" code — so confirm the drain isn't just running slow.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · The causes, in order</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Five causes, most common first.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>1. Clogged filter and sump</h3>
      <p>Food, labels, seeds, and glass grit collect in the filter and the sump below it. Even self-cleaning-filter models can trap debris in the sump. Twist out the filter, rinse it, clear the sump, and reseat it flat and locked.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>2. Pinched or kinked drain hose</h3>
      <p>Samsung's most-cited cause: the dishwasher is sitting on its own drain hose, or the hose is kinked behind the cabinet. Check that it runs free and loops up high before dropping to the drain.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>3. Disposal knockout plug (new install)</h3>
      <p>A new garbage disposal ships with a solid plug sealing the dishwasher drain port. If it wasn't punched out, the dishwasher can't drain — the answer when a Samsung stops draining right after disposal work.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>4. Blocked air gap (common in California)</h3>
      <p>Many California kitchens use an air gap — the chrome cap on the counter — to meet the plumbing code's backflow rule. It clogs and backs water into the dishwasher. Clearing it is a two-minute fix.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>5. Failed drain pump</h3>
      <p>When the whole path is clear and water still stands (or 5C returns), the drain pump has usually failed or its check valve is stuck. Needs a meter and a replacement part.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE CHECKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Five safe steps before you call.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Scoop the standing water</strong> with a cup and sponge so you can reach the filter.</li>
      <li><strong>Clean the filter and sump.</strong> Twist out the filter, rinse it, and clear debris from the sump — watch for broken glass.</li>
      <li><strong>Free the drain hose.</strong> Check behind the unit that it isn't pinched under the dishwasher or kinked, and confirm the high loop.</li>
      <li><strong>Run the garbage disposal</strong> with water, and if it's a new disposal, confirm the knockout plug was removed.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the air gap.</strong> Pop the chrome cap and clean it out. Use only dishwasher detergent, never dish soap.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> filter, sump debris, hose, disposal, air gap, and a 30-second power-cycle are all safe. Don't remove the drain pump or open the control yourself, and mind the glass in the sump. If water still stands after these steps, it's the pump or the control — our job.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 WHEN TO CALL -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clear path, still standing water? It's the pump.</h2>

    <p>Once the filter, hose, disposal, and air gap are verified clear and the tub still holds water (or 5C/SC keeps returning), the drain pump or its check valve is the likely fault, and that needs testing and a replacement part. Our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair carry the common Samsung drain pumps, so a confirmed failure is usually a one-visit fix. We check the upstream path first so we never swap a good pump that was fighting a clog, then quote before any work. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong> Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/dishwasher-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, Orange, and Ventura in most cases.</p>

    <p>Related: the general <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/not-draining/">dishwasher not draining</a> page, the <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/error-codes/">dishwasher error codes</a> guide, and brand coverage on <a href="/brands/samsung-dishwasher-repair/">Samsung dishwasher repair</a>. Full coverage on the <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/">dishwasher repair</a> hub. Nearby: <a href="/beverly-hills/dishwasher-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, <a href="/pasadena/dishwasher-repair/">Pasadena</a>. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Samsung dishwasher not draining: common questions.</h2>
